## Description

Importing a large Arrow string record and calling `DataFrame.Clone()` produces a DataFrame with a corrupted null count and unreadable in-range rows. Exporting the clone back to Arrow also throws.

Reproduced on Microsoft.Data.Analysis **0.23.0**, Apache.Arrow **14.0.2**, Windows x64, .NET 10.

## Reproduction

Create a console app, add `Microsoft.Data.Analysis` version `0.23.0`, and run:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Linq;
using Apache.Arrow;
using Microsoft.Data.Analysis;

const int MaxBufferBytes = 0x7fefffff;
var builder = new StringArray.Builder();
for (int i = 0; i < 7; i++)
    builder.AppendNull();
// Three UTF-8 bytes per character; exactly fills an ML.NET string buffer.
builder.Append(new string('\u20ac', MaxBufferBytes / 3));
builder.Append("i").AppendNull().Append("j").Append("k");
var input = new RecordBatch.Builder()
    .Append("strings", true, builder.Build())
    .Build();
var frame = DataFrame.FromArrowRecordBatch(input);

Console.WriteLine($"Imported rows={frame.Rows.Count}; NullCount={frame.Columns["strings"].NullCount}; row 9 is null={frame[9, 0] is null}");
var clone = frame.Clone();
Console.WriteLine($"Cloned rows={clone.Rows.Count}; NullCount={clone.Columns["strings"].NullCount}");
try
{
    Console.WriteLine($"Cloned row 9 is null={clone[9, 0] is null}");
}
catch (ArgumentOutOfRangeException e)
{
    Console.WriteLine($"Reading cloned row 9: {e.GetType().Name}: {e.Message}");
}
try
{
    var batches = clone.ToArrowRecordBatches().ToArray();
    Console.WriteLine($"Exported {batches.Length} batches");
}
catch (ArgumentException e)
{
    Console.WriteLine($"Exporting clone: {e.GetType().Name}: {e.Message}");
}
```

Allow roughly 8-10 GB of free RAM for the large .NET string, Arrow builder and array, and cloned UTF-8 data. The source contains seven nulls, one string whose UTF-8 representation exactly fills an ML.NET buffer, `"i"`, another null, `"j"`, and `"k"`. Cloning naturally creates a second string buffer.

### Actual output

```text
Imported rows=12; NullCount=8; row 9 is null=True
Cloned rows=12; NullCount=5
Reading cloned row 9: ArgumentOutOfRangeException: Specified argument was out of the range of valid values. (Parameter 'index')
Exporting clone: ArgumentException: Cannot span multiple buffers (Parameter 'numberOfRows')
```

### Expected behavior

Cloning should preserve all 12 values and the null count of 8. `clone[9, 0]` should return `null`, just like the imported DataFrame. Export should return valid Arrow record batches without throwing.

## Relevant implementation defects

All are in [`ArrowStringDataFrameColumn`](https://github.com/dotnet/machinelearning/blob/a83c86a6b1dfd81c95117fd1bf5660ed1c72dd9d/src/Microsoft.Data.Analysis/DataFrameColumns/ArrowStringDataFrameColumn.cs):

- `GetValidityBit` selects the second bitmap for row 9 and obtains buffer-relative index 1, but computes its byte position using the original column-relative index (`9 / 8 == 1` instead of `1 / 8 == 0`). The logical bitmap contains only one byte, so reading the row throws.
- `SetValidityBit` compares buffer-relative `indexInBuffer` with column-wide `Length` when updating `_nullCount`. After entering the second buffer, newly appended values are incorrectly treated as replacements, corrupting the count.
- `GetBufferIndexContainingRowIndex` uses `>` rather than `>=` when crossing a buffer boundary. The first row in the second buffer is assigned to the preceding buffer, corrupting validity metadata during cloning.
- `GetMaxRecordBatchLength` includes the offsets buffer's sentinel entry in its row count. Here it requests 9 rows from the first string buffer, which contains only 8 rows, causing the export exception.

There is also a global/buffer-relative mismatch in `ToArrowArray`: it passes `indexInBuffer` to `GetNullCount`, which calls column-relative `IsValid`. **This high-level repro does not reach the second-buffer export miscount**, because public export throws at the first buffer first. That additional mismatch is a source-level observation, not a behavior established by this run.

## Description

An Arrow record imported with `DataFrame.FromArrowRecordBatch()` can export a null string as a valid empty string when `ToArrowRecordBatches()` divides the DataFrame into multiple batches.

Reproduced on Microsoft.Data.Analysis **0.23.0**, Apache.Arrow **14.0.2**, Windows x64, .NET 10.

## Reproduction

Create a console app, add `Microsoft.Data.Analysis` version `0.23.0`, and run:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Linq;
using Apache.Arrow;
using Microsoft.Data.Analysis;

const int rows = 268_304_384;
var builder = new StringArray.Builder().Reserve(rows);
builder.Append("a");
builder.AppendRange(Enumerable.Repeat("", rows - 2));
builder.AppendNull();
var input = new RecordBatch.Builder()
    .Append("strings", true, builder.Build())
    .Build();
var frame = DataFrame.FromArrowRecordBatch(input);

var numbers = new Int64DataFrameColumn("numbers");
numbers.AppendMany(42, rows);
frame.Columns.Add(numbers);

Console.WriteLine($"Imported rows={frame.Rows.Count}; NullCount={frame.Columns["strings"].NullCount}; final string is null={frame[rows - 1, 0] is null}");
int index = 0;
foreach (var batch in frame.ToArrowRecordBatches())
{
    var strings = batch.Arrays.OfType<StringArray>().Last();
    Console.WriteLine($"Batch {index++}: rows={batch.Length}; last string array length={strings.Length}; NullCount={strings.NullCount}");
    if (strings.Length == 1)
        Console.WriteLine($"Final exported string={(strings.GetString(0) is { } value ? $"\"{value}\"" : "null")}; IsNull={strings.IsNull(0)}");
}
```

This deliberately large input needs several GB of RAM. The numeric column naturally occupies two buffers and forces two record batches. The strings remain in one buffer; their final null is exported as a nonzero-offset, one-row slice. A small, single-batch input would not reach the faulty loop bounds.

### Actual output

```text
Imported rows=268304384; NullCount=1; final string is null=True
Batch 0: rows=268304383; last string array length=268304383; NullCount=0
Batch 1: rows=1; last string array length=1; NullCount=0
Final exported string=""; IsNull=False
```

### Expected behavior

The final string array should have `NullCount == 1`, `IsNull(0) == true`, and `GetString(0) == null`.

The repro selects the last string array because there is a separate bug where `ToArrowRecordBatches()` includes previous batches' arrays in later batches (https://github.com/dotnet/machinelearning/issues/7799). This does not cause the newly exported string array's incorrect null metadata.

## Cause

[`ArrowStringDataFrameColumn.GetNullCount`](https://github.com/dotnet/machinelearning/blob/a83c86a6b1dfd81c95117fd1bf5660ed1c72dd9d/src/Microsoft.Data.Analysis/DataFrameColumns/ArrowStringDataFrameColumn.cs#L338-L347) loops with `i < numberOfRows`, treating a row count as an exclusive end index. It should scan `[startIndex, startIndex + numberOfRows)`.

For the final batch, `startIndex == 268304383` and `numberOfRows == 1`, so no validity bits are checked and the null count is incorrectly zero. `PrimitiveDataFrameColumn<T>.GetNullCount` contains the same loop-bound pattern and should also be considered when fixing this.

## Description

`DataFrame.ToArrowRecordBatches()` accumulates arrays from previous batches. A one-column DataFrame exports its second record batch with one schema field but two arrays, including the first batch's stale array.

This invalid batch cannot be imported using `DataFrame.FromArrowRecordBatch()`, which throws `ArgumentOutOfRangeException`.

Reproduced on Microsoft.Data.Analysis **0.23.0**, Apache.Arrow **14.0.2**, Windows x64, .NET 10. The reproduction uses only public, high-level DataFrame APIs and is independent of Arrow string null-count bugs (https://github.com/dotnet/machinelearning/issues/7800 and https://github.com/dotnet/machinelearning/issues/7801).

## Reproduction

Create a console app, add `Microsoft.Data.Analysis` version `0.23.0`, and run:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Linq;
using Microsoft.Data.Analysis;

var numbers = new Int64DataFrameColumn("numbers");
numbers.AppendMany(42, 268_304_384);
var frame = new DataFrame(numbers);
var batches = frame.ToArrowRecordBatches();

foreach (var (i, batch) in batches.Index())
{
    Console.WriteLine(
        $"Batch {i}: rows={batch.Length}; fields={batch.Schema.FieldsList.Count}; arrays={batch.Arrays.Count()}; array lengths=[{string.Join(", ", batch.Arrays.Select(array => array.Length))}]");

    try
    {
        var roundTrip = DataFrame.FromArrowRecordBatch(batch);
        Console.WriteLine($"Reimported batch {i}: rows={roundTrip.Rows.Count}; columns={roundTrip.Columns.Count}");
    }
    catch (ArgumentOutOfRangeException e)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"Reimporting batch {i}: {e.GetType().Name}: {e.Message}");
    }
}
```

Requires slightly over 2 GB for the numeric buffers and validity bitmaps. The row count naturally creates two numeric buffers and forces two record batches.

### Actual output

```text
Batch 0: rows=268304383; fields=1; arrays=1; array lengths=[268304383]
Reimported batch 0: rows=268304383; columns=1
Batch 1: rows=1; fields=1; arrays=2; array lengths=[268304383, 1]
Reimporting batch 1: ArgumentOutOfRangeException: Index was out of range. Must be non-negative and less than the size of the collection. (Parameter 'index')
```

### Expected behavior

Each batch should contain exactly one array matching its single schema field. Batch 1 should contain only its one-row array, and reimport should produce a one-row, one-column DataFrame containing `42`.

## Cause

[`DataFrame.ToArrowRecordBatches()`](https://github.com/dotnet/machinelearning/blob/a83c86a6b1dfd81c95117fd1bf5660ed1c72dd9d/src/Microsoft.Data.Analysis/DataFrame.Arrow.cs#L173-L207) creates the `arrays` list outside its batching loop and continually appends each batch's arrays without resetting that list.

Each record batch should receive a fresh per-batch collection containing only the current batch's column arrays. Also ensure that continuing enumeration cannot mutate an already yielded batch.
